      "label": "Current Health: Caleb Whitfield is 5 feet 7 inches tall and weighs 150 pounds. His healthcare provider is Dr. Patrick Reyes, and his last routine check-up was in the spring of 2024. His hearing was last evaluated several years ago with no concerns. His eye care provider is Dr. Osei at Clearview Optometric, Rocklin, and his last visit was in September 2023, with results better than 20/20. Caleb Whitfield’s dentist is Dr. Naidu at Family Smiles Dentistry, with his last visit in September 2024, resulting in a successful filling following a routine cleaning.",
      "kind": "prose"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": {
    "approved_case_id": "003",
    "topics": [
      {
        "topic": "narrative",
        "text": "**Pregnancy and Delivery: Caleb Whitfield’s mother was 28 years old at delivery. She received regular prenatal care and Caleb Whitfield was born at 41+ weeks, weighing 8 pounds 13 ounces and measuring 22.5 inches. During pregnancy, the mother experienced a sub chorionic hematoma, used Sudafed and Allegra for allergies, and had one round of antibiotics for a respiratory infection. She also received the H1N1 vaccination, which caused adverse reactions. Caleb Whitfield was delivered vaginally, with skin-to-skin contact after birth. He was sluggish about breastfeeding due to Nubaim administered during labor and required monitoring for low blood sugar and sluggish feeding for two nights post-birth. Due to the low blood sugar, circumcision was delayed several weeks. That procedure was traumatic with healing challenges due to the wound re-adhering.",
        "source": "approved-anonymized case 003 (signed section excerpt)"
      },
      {
        "topic": "narrative",
        "text": "**",
        "source": "approved-anonymized case 003 (signed section excerpt)"
      },
      {
        "topic": "narrative",
        "text": "**Childhood Development: Caleb Whitfield was breastfed until about age 3 and had no significant feeding difficulties. He achieved developmental milestones such as crawling at 9 months, sitting alone at 5.5 months, standing alone at 9-10 months, and walking before 12 months. He said his first word at 10.5 months and was stringing words together by 12 months. He was fully toilet trained somewhat late, by 4.5 years.",
        "source": "approved-anonymized case 003 (signed section excerpt)"
      },
      {
        "topic": "narrative",
        "text": "** Caleb Whitfield has had minimal hospitalizations, including an ambulance ride for a croup attack which was traumatic for Caleb Whitfield. He more recently had an overnight stay in the Emergency Department for apparent dehydration. He was very shaky and couldn’t get warm after a day at the water park last summer. He hadn’t eaten much that day. In the ED, they gave him IV fluids and ran EKG. Caleb Whitfield recovered and was released a few hours later, but vomited all over the ground on the way out, a fairly common reaction to the IV fluids.",
        "source": "approved-anonymized case 003 (signed section excerpt)"
      },
      {
        "topic": "Current Health: Caleb Whitfield is 5 feet 7 inches tall and weighs 150 pounds. His healthcare provider is Dr. Patrick Reyes, and his last routine check-up was in the spring of 2024. His hearing was last evaluated several years ago with no concerns. His eye care provider is Dr. Osei at Clearview Optometric, Rocklin, and his last visit was in September 2023, with results better than 20/20. Caleb Whitfield’s dentist is Dr. Naidu at Family Smiles Dentistry, with his last visit in September 2024, resulting in a successful filling following a routine cleaning.",
        "text": "Caleb Whitfield has no current health concerns and a major shift towards health occurred at age 8-9 years. In his first 7 years, Caleb Whitfield had croup chronically, persistent eczema, strep throat, and ear infections. He was diagnosed at age 5 following a strep infection with Pediatric Autoimmune Neuropsychiatric Disorders Associated with Streptococcal Infections (PANDAS). Illness had led to significant behavioral shifts, including mood swings and compulsive behaviors. Extensive bloodwork in 2017 revealed high strep antibodies despite no symptoms of illness, confirming the diagnosis. Caleb Whitfield underwent long-term antibiotic treatment for about four months, followed by homeopathic remedies, which proved most effective in managing his symptoms. Caleb Whitfield’s sleep patterns were a major problem until about age ten. Caleb Whitfield previously experienced night terrors, night waking and very early morning wakings. He has a big appetite, particularly for carbs, and the family maintains a gluten and dairy-free household. To maintain blood sugar, Caleb Whitfield has to eat regularly. Mood shifts often cue a need for food. Caleb Whitfield is followed by a Dr. Patrick Reyes who specializes in Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, Osteopathic Medicine, Pain Medicine and Public Health & General Preventive Medicine whom he sees regularly. He also sees Dr. Aaron Whitfield for psychiatric care. He takes several medications for diagnosed ADHD, symptoms of mood disorder, and anxiety including 100 mg Strattera for ADHD, 150 mg Zoloft for anxiety, 300 mg Trileptal for sound sensitivity, 25 mg Topomax (reportedly discontinuing soon but was on board to try and balance blood sugar and mood), 1 mg low dose naltrexone (LDN) for inflammation, Vitamin D3+K2 for immune support, and 300 mg Seroquel for sleep and mood swings. Previous medications included Dyanavel, Intuniv, and Geodon, which were discontinued due to various side effects such as motor tics, undesirable mood and/or behavior changes, weight gain, negative thoughts, self harm, and efficacy issues. These experiences highlight the importance of carefully considering Caleb Whitfield's medication history to avoid reintroducing treatments that have previously been ineffective or detrimental.",
        "source": "approved-anonymized case 003 (signed section excerpt)"
      }
    ]
  }
}

